    "Matt.20.13": {
      "path": ".scratch/no-production/pagination-revision/chronology/Matt.20.13.json",
      "sha256": "5b0fa731d39f4f174df709c87993d179f2e9a57647832203f9b14923bb552b18",
      "command": [
        "tools/tpt",
        "scripture-chronology",
        "query",
        "Matt.20.13",
        "--json"
      ],
      "answer": {
        "assertions": [
          {
            "answerability": "answerable",
            "basis": "Jacquier's article \"Gospel of St. Matthew\" in the Catholic Encyclopedia (vol. 10, 1911) reports that \"Eusebius (in his Chronicle), Theophylact, and Euthymius Zigabenus are of opinion that the Gospel of Matthew was written eight years, and Nicephorus Callistus fifteen years, after Christ's Ascension --i.e. about A.D. 38-45.\" The article prefaces the whole survey with the statement that the ancient writers \"are at variance\" and reaches no conclusion.",
            "basis_class": "reported-traditional",
            "date": "38 A.D. to 45 A.D.",
            "derived": false,
            "disposition": "disputed",
            "inherited": true,
            "label": "about A.D. 38-45",
            "note": "\"About A.D. 38-45\" is the encyclopedia's own conversion of the ancients' reckoning in years after the Ascension; the eight-year and fifteen-year figures are what the sources themselves state. The caution that \"we must not expect from the ancients the precision of our modern chronology\" is Durand's, in \"The New Testament\" (Catholic Encyclopedia, vol. 14, New York, 1912), and not this article's; it is cited here as his.",
            "precision": "interval",
            "profile": "catholic-traditional-v1",
            "relation": "composition",
            "reporting_exception": null,
            "scope": "Matt",
            "sources": [
              "artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-10.new-york-1911.newadvent-10057a-e7b6ccef",
              "artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-14.new-york-1912.newadvent-14530a-0a19aa2c"
            ],
            "subject": "composition.gospel-of-matthew",
            "title": "The Gospel of St Matthew"
          },
          {
            "answerability": "answerable",
            "basis": "The same article draws a second date from the tradition of the dispersal of the Apostles: \"following a certain tradition (admittedly not too reliable), the Apostles separated twelve years after the Ascension, hence the Gospel would have been written about the year 40-42\". Eusebius is its authority for the peg, since \"According to Eusebius, Matthew wrote his Gospel in Hebrew when he left Palestine.\"",
            "basis_class": "traditional-catholic",
            "date": "40 A.D. to 42 A.D.",
            "derived": false,
            "disposition": "disputed",
            "inherited": true,
            "label": "about the year 40-42",
            "note": "The article itself calls the twelve-year tradition \"admittedly not too reliable\" and immediately offers the alternative reckoning that produces about the year 60-68 from the same peg.",
            "precision": "interval",
            "profile": "catholic-traditional-v1",
            "relation": "composition",
            "reporting_exception": null,
            "scope": "Matt",
            "sources": [
              "artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-10.new-york-1911.newadvent-10057a-e7b6ccef"
            ],
            "subject": "composition.gospel-of-matthew",
            "title": "The Gospel of St Matthew"
          },
          {
            "answerability": "answerable",
            "basis": "For modern opinion the same article states: \"In our day opinion is rather divided. Catholic critics, in general, favour the years 40-45\". This is the position it attributes to Catholic critics as a body, as against the individual figures it goes on to list.",
            "basis_class": "reported-traditional",
            "date": "40 A.D. to 45 A.D.",
            "derived": false,
            "disposition": "disputed",
            "inherited": true,
            "label": "the years 40-45",
            "note": "The same sentence records individual Catholic departures from 40-45 \u2014 Patrizi 36-39, Aberle 37, Belser 41-42, Conely 40-50, Schafer 50-51, and Hug, Reuschl, Schanz and Rose 60-67 \u2014 which are not authored separately here. The article also fixes its own upper bound: \"Matthew must have written his Gospel before the year 70\". The long list of Protestant and rationalist figures in the same paragraph (Renan later than 85, Baur about 130-34, and the rest) is reported there in order to be refuted and is no part of this profile.",
            "precision": "interval",
            "profile": "catholic-traditional-v1",
            "relation": "composition",
            "reporting_exception": null,
            "scope": "Matt",
            "sources": [
              "artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-10.new-york-1911.newadvent-10057a-e7b6ccef"
            ],
            "subject": "composition.gospel-of-matthew",
            "title": "The Gospel of St Matthew"
          },
          {
            "answerability": "answerable",
            "basis": "The same article's alternative reading of the same peg: \"following Eusebius (Church History III.5.2), it is possible to fix the definitive departure of the Apostles about the year 60, in which event the writing of the Gospel would have taken place about the year 60-68.\" The article presents this beside the 40-42 reckoning without choosing between them.",
            "basis_class": "traditional-catholic",
            "date": "60 A.D. to 68 A.D.",
            "derived": false,
            "disposition": "disputed",
            "inherited": true,
            "label": "about the year 60-68",
            "note": "",
            "precision": "interval",
            "profile": "catholic-traditional-v1",
            "relation": "composition",
            "reporting_exception": null,
            "scope": "Matt",
            "sources": [
              "artifact.catholic-encyclopedia.volume-10.new-york-1911.newadvent-10057a-e7b6ccef"
            ],
            "subject": "composition.gospel-of-matthew",
            "title": "The Gospel of St Matthew"
          },
          {
